text:
maria: Heads-up — saw a spike in inbound routing latency this morning. ~10% of leads stalled at the enrichment step for >5m. HubSpot webhook shows delivered but enrichment job never returned :grimacing:

dan: thx — looking. I can see a cluster of 502s hitting the enrichment service between 08:12–08:18 UTC. HubSpot retried a couple times, then we fell back to RR and some leads got reassigned incorrectly.

ashwin: can we add a lightweight pre-check (routing-lint) that validates enriched payload schema + ttl before letting round-robin claim the lead? if enrichment times out or payload is malformed, we should hold it in a retry queue instead of handing to sales.

lina: agree. that would reduce noisy owner churn. quick questions: 1) who owns the lint function? 2) how do we surface metrics to PM and SDRs?

maria: @ashwin can you own a tiny lint lambda? ops can add an alert. PMs want SLA delta numbers — propose temporarily extend claim TTL from 2m -> 10m while we roll.

ashwin: I can spin up a PoC today. idea: HubSpot -> webhook -> routing-lint (validate) -> enrichment job -> mark "enriched:true" -> route to RR/SF. If lint fails, we post back to hubspot with status=deferred and push to a dead-letter retry queue. Keeping it synchronous-ish reduces races.

ashwin: sample Node PoC (very small):
```
// routing-lint.js (express)
app.post('/webhook', async (req, res) => {
  const payload = req.body;
  if (!payload.email || !payload.lead_id) return res.status(400).send('bad schema');
  if (payload.enrichment && payload.enrichment.age_seconds > 600) {
    // too old, defer
    await pushToRetryQueue(payload);
    return res.status(202).send('deferred');
  }
  // quick sanity checks
  if (payload.enrichment && typeof payload.enrichment.score !== 'number') {
    await pushToRetryQueue(payload);
    return res.status(202).send('deferred');
  }
  // mark and forward
  await forwardToEnrichment(payload);
  res.status(200).send('accepted');
});
```

maria: nice — can we also add an immediate heartbeat ack back to HubSpot when we receive the webhook so HubSpot marks it as "processing"? that prevents RR from picking it up if their connector polls raw un-enriched rows.

dan: yes. HubSpot supports a synchronous 200 response and then we can call the HubSpot API to set a property like `redwood_processing:true`. That would need a short TTL and a cleanup job.

ops-signal: if we do this, add an observability metric: `routing_lint.defer_count`, `routing_lint.accept_latency_ms`, and an alert when `enrichment_latency.p95 > 3m` or `routing_lint.defer_rate > 1%` in 15m window. PagerDuty channel = infra-oncall.

lina: on SLAs — marketing/ops want numbers we can share with sales: propose a canary where 5% of inbound traffic goes through lint+heartbeat for 48h, measure claim stability (owner flip rate) and lead-to-first-touch latency. If stable, roll to 100% and shrink TTL back to 2m.

maria: rollout plan: 1) ashwin deploy PoC to staging, run synthetic payloads. 2) ops add metrics/alerts. 3) start 5% canary Monday 09:00 UTC. 4) check owner-flip metric at 6h and 24h. 5) if owner flips down <1% and p95 latency < 2m, expand.

ashwin: i'll add a small integration test and a `curl` command to simulate failing enrichment: 
```
curl -X POST https://staging-routing-lint.redwood.example/webhook \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"lead_id":"abc","email":"test+f@redwood.com","enrichment":{"age_seconds":1200}}'
```

dan: nice. also let's add a Kibana query to find past impacted leads: `event:enrichment AND status:timeout AND @timestamp:[now-1d TO now]` so PMs can surface examples to SDRs.

maria: action items: @ashwin = PoC + tests (ETA EOD), @dan = HubSpot property + cleanup job (ETA Tues), @ops-signal = alerts/dashboards (ETA Tues), @lina = coordinate canary comms with SDRs + update SLA docs. I'll post a short playbook after the canary.

hubspot-bot: webhook delivery log: 08:13:42 UTC — 14 events delivered for contact-sync, 3 retried, 2 failed (502).

lina: thanks all — if you hit blockers ping me. :thumbsup:
